  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nAn area of showers and thunderstorms extends across parts of IA\nearly this morning. This activity will continue moving east/\nsoutheast through mid morning, though a gradual weakening trend\nis expected, especially on the eastern/leading edge with quite a\nbit of uncertainty for how much of this precipitation will reach\nthe southwest cwa this morning. Have capped pops at likely and\neven this may be too high. Eventually, as the main wave moves\nacross northern IL this afternoon, additional showers and a few\nthunderstorms will be possible from midday through late \nafternoon with precipitation chances ending by sunset as any \nlingering activity shifts east over Lake Michigan.\n\nHigh temperatures today will be tricky with extensive high/mid\nlevel cloud cover expected. Its possible high temps may be\nwarmer than the expected lower 70s across the northern cwa but\nmaintained those temps for now, with highs in the mid/upper 70s\nsouth of I-80.\n\nHighs on Saturday will likely reach the upper 70s/lower 80s for\nmost areas, but cooler along the IL Lake Michigan shore with\nsouth/southeast winds. The winds on Saturday may gust into the\n25-30 mph range during the afternoon.\n\nIncreasing clouds are expected Saturday afternoon into Saturday\nevening as the remnants of Isaias lift north into the Ohio \nValley. There will likely be a sharp cutoff to the precipitation\non the northwest side of this system so the challenge will be \nhow far northwest does the system move. Still quite a bit of \nuncertainty though the trend seems to support the bulk of the \nprecipitation remaining generally east of I-57 (or further \neast). Did not make any changes to the blended pops which \nmaintain a dry forecast for all but the far southeast cwa. Winds\nare expected to have an easterly component Sunday morning and \nperhaps all day Sunday, keeping temperatures cooler near Lake \nMichigan but with some sunshine possible midday in the \nafternoon, highs could easily reach the mid/upper 70s inland.\n\nAnother warm day is expected Monday with high temperatures in\nthe lower to mid 80s expected. Its possible a few locations may\ntag upper 80s Monday afternoon. A cold front is then expected \nto move across the area Tuesday morning, though there remains \nuncertainty on timing. A slower arrival will likely allow for a \nvery mild Monday night with lows possibly only in the 60s and \nthen highs rebounding into the upper 70s/lower 80s on Tuesday, \nsouth of the frontal boundary. There will also be a chance of \nshowers and possibly a few thunderstorms during this time period\nthough quite a bit of uncertainty for coverage.\n\nThe cold front is then expected to stall as low pressure\ndevelops over the Plains and lifts back north as a warm front\nThursday/Thursday evening, as the low deepens and races\nnortheast to the northern Great Lakes by Friday morning. Shower\nand thunderstorms chances will be possible as the warm front\nlifts north across the area and then again as a strong cold \nfront moves across the area Thursday night or Friday morning. \nWhile its just beyond the end of the 7 day period, it appears \nthere could be much colder temperatures for at least a few days\nbehind this cold front and going into next weekend. cms",
